avors Republicans by about a single point. The incumbent president is term-limited, making this an open-seat contest with no incumbency advantage for either party. No general-election polls, expert ratings, or fundraising figures are available yet, so the outlook rests on the state's underlying fundamentals.\n\nRecent coverage has focused on the Democratic primary calendar, where Nevada lost its early voting slot to South Carolina, and on early positioning by prospective 2028 candidates. Those developments shape the nomination race more than the November outcome. With the state this evenly divided, modest shifts in turnout or the national climate could decide the winner, and uncertainty stays wide until real campaign data arrives.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.4561, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.4622, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.4656, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.4628, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.4646, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.4672, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.4612, "margin": -1.0}]}